  "narrative": "Synthesis stage assessment and final report for this iteration.\n\n1) Due validation reassessment: H38 (outcome_006, feature_021=1 vs 0, direction positive) is accepted and closed. Discovery R29 gives signed estimate +0.8019 [0.7823,0.8216]; independent validation R132 gives +0.7999 [0.7689,0.8309] at alpha 0.00385. Both intervals exclude the null and lie far above the approximate 10% absolute-scale clinical reference for outcome_006. The effect is enormous relative to the outcome scale and is therefore a robust dataset-specific association: feature_021=1 is associated with a much less negative outcome_006 CRISPR dependency score, i.e. weaker dependency. This conclusion does not imply causality or external generalizability.\n\n2) Supported scientific conclusion in this dataset: The dominant supported result is the feature_021/outcome_006 association in H38. The additional accepted outcome_006 feature_021 subgroup claims should be read mainly as internal consistency checks showing broadness across many subgroups, not as separate predictors. No new independent secondary predictor has been established in this dataset.\n\n3) Negative and unresolved lines: Several discovery-positive secondary results failed independent validation or remain underpowered: H36 outcome_015 feature_021 within feature_002=0 validated near zero (+0.0004 [-0.0428,0.0436]); H41 outcome_005 feature_021 within feature_002=0 validated +0.0116 [-0.0327,0.0560]; H124 outcome_014 feature_063 validated opposite and wide; H28 overall outcome_014 feature_021 validated +0.0098 [-0.0516,0.0712]. These are not supported as clinically meaningful, although the validation intervals do not entirely rule out smaller effects. Many other screens are null or only weakly positive: outcome_006 feature_012, feature_050, feature_020, feature_052, feature_057, and interaction tests by age or nominal classifiers do not establish clinically meaningful differences. H154 outcome_015 feature_063 positive is rejected because the upper interval is below the positive benchmark, a useful negative cross-outcome test.\n\n4) Direction-reversal evidence and borderline effects: Some rejected positive claims show opposite-direction discovery effects that are worth noting but not accepted: H49 outcome_016 feature_021 points toward stronger dependency when recast in the opposite direction, but the lower bound is below the clinical benchmark; H108/H114 outcome_005 feature_045 and H73/H75 outcome_006 feature_052 show similar suggestive reverse associations with lower bounds below benchmark. H153 outcome_014 feature_021 interaction with feature_045=1 is the most interesting secondary signal: signed interaction +0.1030 [0.0027,0.2033]. It excludes zero, but the lower bound is below the benchmark and there is no remaining validation budget, so I do not accept it as clinically meaningful. The registered simple stratum effects H155/H156 are the most useful next internal analyses to determine whether the interaction is driven by a large feature_021 effect in one feature_045 stratum.\n\n5) Most informative next analysis: Within this dataset, the highest-value remaining analysis is the focused simple effect of feature_021 on outcome_014 within feature_045=1 and feature_045=0 (already registered as H155/H156), because it follows directly from H153 and tests a concrete, hypothesis-specific question. More broadly, the most scientifically informative next step is external or functional validation of the robust feature_021-outcome_006 association, since causal interpretation and generalization are not established from these data. Further opportunistic predictor screening is less informative than validating the dominant association or testing the small number of already-nominated borderline secondary effects.\n\nOverall status: one very robust dataset-specific finding (H38); many negative or unsupported secondary associations; several borderline direction-reversal or interaction effects remain unresolved; no causal or generalizable biomarker claims are made.",
